+FIXED - cluster ci-84e380615213284e: `CarlaVst2Discovery.cpp` read a `QFileInfo` date without `<QDateTime>` in scope
+
+Verdict: **FIXED (REAL)**. The hosted `build / windows-arm64` failure is exactly
+reproduced on this box against the real Qt 5.15.19 headers, and fixed by one include.
+It is a first-party (fork-NEW) translation unit, not CI infrastructure.
+
+## The failure
+
+Job `run-43537465762-job-132269830963`, train 050/m0-train-29 at
+`7798296550396e87861e7e98c43397dcf854f506` (this checkout's HEAD):
+
+```
+plugins/CarlaBase/CarlaVst2Discovery.cpp:204:31: error: calling 'lastModified' with
+    incomplete return type 'QDateTime'
+plugins/CarlaBase/CarlaVst2Discovery.cpp:204:45: error: member access into incomplete
+    type 'QDateTime'
+2 errors generated.
+```
+
+`CarlaVst2Discovery.cpp:204` (`:205` after the fix) is
+
+```cpp
+const qint64 modified = info.lastModified().toMSecsSinceEpoch();
+```
+
+The file included `<QDir>`, `<QFileInfo>`, `<QHash>`, `<QMutex>`, `<QProcess>` … but not
+`<QDateTime>`. That compiles on this box only because Qt 6.12's `qfileinfo.h` includes
+`QtCore/qdatetime.h` (and `qdirlisting.h`, reached from `<QDir>`); Qt 5.15's
+`qfileinfo.h` only forward-declares `class QDateTime;` and declares the accessors out
+of line (`/usr/include/qt/QtCore/qfileinfo.h:53,139-145`), which is exactly the clang
+diagnostic in the job.
+
+## Reproduction (real Qt 5.15.19 headers, no shadow)
+
+```
+/usr/bin/c++ -std=gnu++20 -fsyntax-only $(pkg-config --cflags Qt5Widgets Qt5Xml Qt5Network) \
+  -Iinclude -Ibuild -Ibuild/src -Ibuild/plugins/CarlaBase \
+  -Iplugins/CarlaBase/carla/source -Iplugins/CarlaBase/carla/source/includes \
+  -Iplugins/CarlaBase/carla/source/backend -Iplugins/CarlaBase/carla/source/utils \
+  -Iplugins/CarlaBase/CarlaConfig -Isrc/3rdparty/weakjack/weakjack \
+  -Isrc/3rdparty/jack2/common -Isrc/3rdparty/ringbuffer/include -Ibuild/src/3rdparty \
+  -Isrc/3rdparty/lua -Isrc/3rdparty/luabridge plugins/CarlaBase/CarlaVst2Discovery.cpp
+```
+
+| Stage | Exit | Log |
+|---|---:|---|
+| original code | **1** — `CarlaVst2Discovery.cpp:204:50: error: invalid use of incomplete type 'class QDateTime'` | `41-qt5-real-compile-red.log` |
+| with the fix | **0** | `40-qt5-real-compile-green.log` |
+
+The same command compiled **every** other first-party translation unit that calls a
+`QFileInfo` date accessor (`lastModified`, `birthTime`, `metadataChangeTime`,
+`lastRead`, `fileTime`) — 27 of them, 20 product and 7 test translation units — and
+only this one failed. That is the completeness check for the "every caller and sibling"
+rule: `src/core/PluginFactoryScanDetail.h:104,133` also calls `lastModified()` without
+including `<QDateTime>`, but both of its including translation units
+(`PluginFactory.cpp:28`, `PluginFactoryBackgroundScan.cpp`) include `<QDateTime>` by
+name and build on Qt 5 (verified: both exit 0).
+
+## The fix
+
+`plugins/CarlaBase/CarlaVst2Discovery.cpp` — one line, `<QDateTime>` beside `<QDir>` in
+the file's own include block. No behaviour, ABI, command, undo or realtime change: the
+file is the GUI/control-thread VST2 discovery scanner (its header comment states that
+nothing there touches audio), and the include only names a type already used. It is a
+fork-NEW file (`tests/fork-sources.txt:3534`), so no divergence-ledger entry is needed.
+
+## The regression test
+
+`tests/Qt5QFileInfoDateCheck.py`, registered as CTest `Qt5QFileInfoDateCheck`
+(`tests/CMakeLists.txt`, beside the other Qt 5 portability checks), registered in
+`tests/fork-sources.txt`.
+
+The defect is invisible on a Qt 6.12 host, so the test does not trust this box's
+headers. It compiles each candidate translation unit through its **own**
+`build/compile_commands.json` command line against a QtCore/qfileinfo.h (and
+qdirlisting.h, which `<QDir>` reaches on Qt 6) in the failing shape: the two
+QDateTime/QTimeZone includes removed, `QDateTime` forward-declared and the accessors
+declared out of line — the exact Qt 5 header shape, derived from the real header so it
+cannot drift. On a Qt whose real header already has that shape (the hosted Qt 5 jobs)
+it uses the real header and builds no shadow. Test translation units get their own
+`.moc` from the build's moc, as automoc does.
+
+Three properties keep it from being a tautology:
+
+* a known-bad fixture (date accessor, no `<QDateTime>`) must be **rejected with the
+  CI's own QDateTime diagnostic** before any product file is judged — the run prints
+  whether it was;
+* a known-good fixture (same code with the include) must compile;
+* a translation unit that does not build on this host for an unrelated reason is
+  reported SKIP; a baseline compile whose diagnostic is the CI's own - an **incomplete
+  QDateTime** - is FAIL, never SKIP. On the failing Qt 5 / macOS Qt 6 header shape
+  nothing supplies QDateTime, so that error is raised by the *baseline* compile: the
+  sweep would have passed while the hosted job was red, which is the hole the first
+  review found (`tests/Qt5QFileInfoDateCheck.py:288-290` at `035e3bcae9`). The
+  classifier is proved by a self-test on synthetic compiler output both ways: the two
+  spellings of the incomplete-type error (clang's "calling 'lastModified' with
+  incomplete return type" and gcc's "invalid use of incomplete type") are FAIL, while an
+  unrelated compiler error, a missing-header error and a different QDateTime error
+  (e.g. no matching `QDateTime::fromString`) remain skippable.
+
+The registered test sweeps **twice** where a Qt 5 development tree exists: once through
+this build's Qt 6 headers with the shadow shape, and once against the real Qt 5 root
+(`pkg-config --variable=includedir Qt5Core`, overridable with `--qt5-root`), with every
+recorded Qt 6 include root rewritten to its Qt 5 counterpart. The second sweep is the
+hosted configuration itself: there the failing translation unit's BASELINE compile says
+`invalid use of incomplete type 'class QDateTime'`, and the test now fails on it.
+
+It needs no second toolchain, no network, no clock: the only inputs are the recorded
+compile commands, the real Qt headers and the fixtures.
+
